he committed debounced level, a candidate level, and a counter. On each timer ISR, read the pin once. If the raw sample equals the candidate level, increment the counter; if it differs, load the new level into the candidate and reset the counter to 1 (this sample is the first of a new run). When the counter reaches 5 — five consecutive ticks showing the same level — copy the candidate into the committed debounced state and, if it changed, set an edge flag (pressed/released) for the main loop or RTOS task to consume. The same rule runs continuously, so both the press and the release edges are debounced identically; the ISR does only a pin read, a compare and a counter update, and all application logic stays outside the interrupt.\n\nThe five confirming samples are 1 ms apart, so the first and the fifth are separated by (5 − 1) × 1 ms = 4 ms, and any sample of the opposite level inside that window resets the counter and restarts the run. Consequently the longest bounce this scheme tolerates is 4 ms: contact chatter lasting up to 4 ms can never yield five agreeing samples and therefore cannot produce a false press or release. Bounce longer than 4 ms is not passed through either — it simply keeps resetting the counter and delays the accepted transition until the contacts have been quiet for a full 4 ms window. This figure assumes the 1 kHz ISR meets its 1 ms deadline; interrupt latency or jitter much smaller than 1 ms does not change the sample count, but a missed or delayed tick stretches the effective window and must be avoided by keeping the ISR short and its priority above non-critical work.", "domain_concerns": [], "blocked_on": [], "not_stated": [], "results": [{"ask": 5, "value": 4.0, "unit": "ms"}]}, "blocked_check": {"blocked": [], "none_stated": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "nothing blocked on"}, "answer_end": {"cut": false, "signs": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "answer ends whole"}, "coverage": {"missed": ["in two short par